AB  - This chapter discusses the application of artificial intelligence (AI) to the field of nutrigenomics of livestock, redefining nutrition as both stationary and dynamic provisioning to systems with multi-objective stewardship. It describes the biological underpinnings of gene-nutrient, epigenetic, microbiome, and metabolic regulation, followed by an outline of AI architectures, multimodal representation learning, graph and causal models, hybrid mechanistic-data fusion, temporal and reinforcement learning, which map heterogeneous asynchronous omics, sensor, and environmental streams to predictive and prescriptive nutritional strategies. It focuses on uncertainty quantification, semantic interoperability, ontologies, and federated structures that can be used to perform privacy-preserving cross-farm generalization. Multi-objective optimization is associated with productivity, health, welfare, emission reduction, and economic resilience, whereas ethical governance deals with data sovereignty, algorithmic fairness, transparency, and environmental footprint (Green AI). It roots a visionary agenda of nutritional decision support based on continuity, equity, and sustainability on digital twins, participatory databases, and continuous learning loops.
