AB  - For Indigenous Peoples of Abya Yala (the Guna Peoples’ term for the Americas), particularly among the Kara and Kichwa Peoples of the Pan-Andean–Amazonian biocultural region, data are not merely a knowledge resource. Data are an extension of Khipus Panaka (Indigenous data authority), treading the data lifecycle — genealogical memory held within customary law and collective responsibility. This paper presents the Kara::Kichwa Data Sovereignty Framework, a legal instrument developed through rights to story and place and Indigenous-informed scope review. This allows engagement with external Indigenous data governance resources while counteracting ‘intellectual gentrification’ and the systemic invisibility of Andean-Amazonian Indigenous Peoples in the digital transformation. The framework codifies five customary legal–ethical pillars: Kamachŷ, Ayľu-ľaktapak kamachŷ, Ťanťanakû, Wiľay-panka-tantay, and Sumak kawsay to guide the translation of principle-level data ethics to operational procedures on the data system lifecycle. While this framework arises from Kara::Kichwa customary law, its pillars are enacted and sustained through Pacha (the indissoluble unity of space, time, consciousness, dimension). It outlines how its governance logics serve as a reference point for Indigenous data authority renascences in Latin America and the Caribbean. We use the concept of ‘renascences’ (plural, not singular) as the act of coming back into being on one’s own terms: not a return to a frozen past or imposed, but a re-emergence from the root system that never died.
ER  - 

