AB  - Personal health data are increasingly generated by wearables and distributed across heterogeneous clinical systems, yet effective patient sovereignty over storage, sharing, and semantic access remains limited. Existing blockchain-based health data management solutions improve provenance but remain institution-centric, with weak support for wearable participation, encrypted semantic retrieval, and fine-grained disclosure control. This paper presents PersonalHealthChain, a patient-centric framework that integrates M-RAFT, a vitality-aware adaptive consensus protocol for heterogeneous devices, a blockchain–IPFS storage plane for integrity anchoring and encrypted off-chain storage, and a patient-owned Personal Health Knowledge Graph (PHKG) for concept- and time-scoped access. Experiments show that M-RAFT remains within 4%–6% of standard Raft under stable conditions, while achieving up to 51% lower election latency and 102% higher throughput under heterogeneity. Meanwhile, annual ledger growth stays below 16 MB under heavy workloads, and PHKG-based semantic access reduces query latency by 68% and decrypted data volume by 94% compared with file-level access. These results demonstrate the practicality of PersonalHealthChain for secure and patient-controlled health data sharing.
ER  - 

