AB  - Artificial intelligence (AI) is reshaping higher education and creating new opportunities for international collaboration. Although China–Africa AI cooperation has expanded rapidly, limited attention has been paid to the institutional mechanisms through which it contributes to university transformation. Drawing on a qualitative synthesis of recent literature, policy documents, and institutional reports, this article develops an analytical framework to examine China–Africa AI cooperation through technology transfer, absorptive capacity, knowledge co-production, and governance. The analysis argues that AI cooperation should be understood not simply as the transfer of technologies or digital infrastructure, but as an institutional process that strengthens universities' research capability and innovation capacity. It also identifies governance challenges, including technological dependency, data sovereignty, institutional disparities, and financial sustainability. The study extends technology transfer theory to the context of South–South AI cooperation and provides an analytical framework for understanding AI-enabled higher education transformation and informing future empirical research.
