AB  - The incorporation of digital sovereignty concepts into AI systems is crucial for ethical data usage and technological independence, especially in agriculture. This study examines how these principles support deep learning–based plant disease monitoring systems. We used several convolutional neural networks with transfer learning on the PlantVillage dataset containing images of various plant diseases, using AlexNet, VGG, ResNet, and DenseNet as pre-trained models. Our objective was to build a plant disease detection system supporting both accuracy and ethics. We evaluate data sovereignty compliance and performance indicators (accuracy, F1-score, precision, recall). We confirmed that the performance of the model and the principle of data sovereignty are highly correlated. We utilized the different models, such as AlexNet, DenseNet121, ResNet50, CNN, VGG16, as well as the basics of NN. We found the DenseNet model is suitable for soil fertility in FL settings. DenseNet121 achieved 99.657% accuracy and an F1-score of 0.99656, enabling secure disease identification while preserving data ownership. Overall, this study shows that transfer learning can include digital sovereignty criteria without affecting analytical performance. DenseNet121 achieved 99.657% accuracy and an F1-score of 0.99656, enabling secure disease identification while preserving data ownership. Overall, this study shows that transfer learning can include digital sovereignty criteria without affecting analytical performance.
