KW  - Blockchain
KW  - Food Traceability
KW  - Hyperledger Fabric
KW  - EPCIS
KW  - Supply Chain
KW  - Smart Contracts
AB  - The increasing complexity of global food supply chains has created significant challenges in maintaining end-to-end traceability and transparency while preserving commercial confidentiality. Traditional traceability systems rely on fragmented, siloed approaches that lack interoperability and fail to provide adequate visibility during food safety incidents. This paper presents a novel blockchain-based framework that addresses these challenges by integrating Hyperledger Fabric’s permissioned blockchain architecture with Electronic Product Code Information Services (EPCIS) data standards. Our proposed solution implements a dual channel architecture that enables controlled migration of traceability data from private to public channels, balancing transparency requirements with commercial sensitivity needs. The system captures EPCIS events across four critical phases of the food supply chain, using IoT sensors and standardized data formats. Smart contracts orchestrate the complete data lifecycle, from initial capture in organization-specific private channels to selective publication in shared public channels based on business relationships and authorization policies. The framework demonstrates how blockchain technology can create immutable, auditable records while maintaining data sovereignty for individual supply chain participants. The integration of EPCIS standards ensures semantic interoperability across diverse stakeholders, while the selective publication mechanism enables controlled transparency for regulatory compliance and consumer trust. This research contributes to understanding the technical implementation and governance considerations for enterprise blockchain deployments in multi-organizational supply chain networks, providing a foundation for scalable, privacy-preserving traceability solutions in the global food system.
ER  - 

