KW  - Diversified payment
KW  - Public accountability
KW  - Blockchain
AB  - In next-generation distributed computing environments, such as Web 3.0, the Internet of Things, and Data-as-a-Service infrastructures, data trading has become an important means of facilitating data circulation and realizing data value. Blockchain is widely adopted in data trading because of its transparency, immutability, and smart-contract-based enforcement. However, secure and fair blockchain-based data trading remains challenging. Although Ciphertext-Policy Attribute-Based Encryption (CP-ABE) provides data confidentiality and fine-grained access control, mutual distrust between trading parties requires the entire trading ciphertext to be publicly verifiable. Moreover, existing schemes lack flexible payment mechanisms that preserve data sovereignty. To address these challenges, we propose a publicly accountable data trading framework for Web 3.0 environments. We construct a composite trading policy that separates buyer access control from payment authorization while supporting both pay-per-use and subscription-based trading. We then extend linear secret-sharing scheme (LSSS) to support public verifiability for all LSSS shares. Building on this, we construct a publicly accountable CP-ABE scheme and further achieve full public verifiability of the trading ciphertext. We also design publicly verifiable pay-per-use and subscription keys. The framework is implemented on Ethereum, where smart contracts enforce public verification and payment escrow. Formal security analysis and experimental evaluation demonstrate the security, feasibility, and efficiency of the proposed framework.
ER  - 

