AB  - Digital sovereignty has become a global concern due to the exponential increase and dominance of digital services, in which users seek control over their digital assets. Federated learning emerges to maintain data sovereignty while training models for secured learning. Integrating federated learning with computer-assisted pronunciation training (CAPT) systems for children not only assist children to develop proper pronunciation skills but also to ensure their data sovereignty and privacy of their voice data, and thus improve their language skills in a controlled and secured environment. This empowers parents/guardians with sovereignty over their children’s voice features without an adult’s supervision. CAPT systems for children encounter extra challenges than adults, in terms of the different acoustic features resulted from the uncontrolled real-world environments with noises, and the variations caused by children’s behavior, i.e., getting closer/farther from microphones, moving around, complicating pronunciation assessment especially with Arabic language. In this paper, the Federated Learning-based Arabic Pronunciation Analyzer for Children (FL-APAC) system is proposed to ensure data sovereignty during children learning in real-world noisy environments. The system uses 2D spectrogram representations of pronounced words generated on the child’s device. This abstracts the detailed audio characteristics to achieve data sovereignty and provides secured visual representation of sound frequencies, reducing the risk of exposing their sensitive vocal features. Vision transformers (ViT) are utilized to handle children’s behaviors, while the encoder-decoder codec (EnCodec) handles occlusion, background, and structure noise types. A real-world dataset was used to conduct our experiments, having 16 Arabic words pronounced by children of 7-12 years old. Our proposed system outperformed the ViT trained on clean data augmented via common data augmentation techniques by 11.6%, achieving an average accuracy of 78.88% for handling both different children’s behaviors and noise types in 25ms while maintaining data sovereignty over the processed data.
ER  - 

