AB  - Federated learning (FL) trains machine learning models across decentralized data silos without exchanging raw data, responding to concerns over privacy, data sovereignty, and regulatory compliance. Progress is fragmented across architectures, optimization strategies, and threat models, and the convergence of FL with foundation models has outpaced existing surveys. This narrative, critical review synthesizes selected FL research, combining landmark and recent literature; it is neither systematic nor exhaustive, and its source selection was not protocol-based. We organize architectures along three axes (coordination topology, deployment scale, and trust boundary), treating hybrid systems as compositions of axis values. We classify more than fifteen optimization algorithms under a single rule, trace each judgment to the few controlled comparisons that exist, and label the remainder qualitative. We code the evidence maturity of ten application areas: healthcare is best evidenced, whereas most other areas rest mainly on surveys and single method studies. We pair privacy and security threats with defenses, distinguishing record-level from client-level differential privacy and noting that secure aggregation tolerates client dropouts but can be circumvented by a malicious server. Across the sources examined, privacy, utility, efficiency, robustness, and fairness are repeatedly reported to be in tension, and no single method we discuss resolves them together; we record the evidence basis of each trade-off without estimating an empirical Pareto frontier. We propose twenty research gaps and thirty future directions. Classes, maturity levels, and horizons reflect the authors’ interpretation of the cited sources.
