AB  - In the digital age, information has become a key determinant of national strength, economic development, and institutional confidence. However, traditional legal doctrines of sovereignty are becoming increasingly inadequate for regulating data mobility that is not confined to any particular geographical location. This paper proposes a risk-based, responsive framework in which data sovereignty is redefined as a quantifiable, persistently evolving governance potential. The framework integrates benchmarking (SDI), dynamic assessment (DSAM), and institutional coordination. Their integration creates a feedback-driven system, which connects national preparedness (macro-level) to (micro-level) dataset risk management. Institutionalization of the framework entails establishing a proposed National Data Sovereignty Authority (NDSA) to coordinate, certify, and adjust policies. A comparative study of the EU, US, BRICS, OECD, and GCC settings reveals the model's flexibility and global applicability. Building on this insight, the model defines sovereignty as dynamic rather than static and, by providing evidence-based guidance, gives governments and industries a systematic way to secure interests, build interoperability, and establish trust in cross-border data flows.
