AB  - The open banking model embodies the principle of open innovation, enabling third-party systems to offer innovative services by accessing banking/financial data via application programming interfaces with the customer's consent. The open banking model also emphasizes data sovereignty, meaning the customer is the real custodian of data, not the service provider (Banks, etc.). In India, the phenomenon of open innovation and data sovereignty is established through the Data Empowerment and Protection Architecture (DEPA). DEPA enables data sharing in a secure, organized, and consent-driven way. The Government of India implemented the DEPA framework, first in the banking domain through the Account Aggregation (AA) model. The AA model acts as a consent manager, serving as a middle agent between Financial Information Providers (FIPs) (e.g., banks, IRDAI, etc.) and Financial Information Users (FIUs) (third-party service providers) to enable secure data sharing with customer consent. This paper aims to enhance the DEPA framework in the open banking ecosystem by incorporating ZKP (Zero-Knowledge Proof) algorithms to address data privacy concerns. In the proposed framework, instead of transferring the raw data (in encrypted form), the new model will share proofs generated from the data using ZKP algorithms. The proposed system has two different versions: i) ZK-SNARK-based Proof Generation for Preserving Data Privacy in the Open Banking Model (ZPGPDP_OBM), and ii) Zero-Knowledge-Service Provider (ZK-SD) assisted proof generation for preserving Data Privacy (ZK_SD_PGPDP_OBM). The proposed systems are implemented using a hybrid model (Python and Go) by considering two different cases: i) a digital lending system and ii) an insurance system. Overall, the performance analysis of the proposed framework, ZK_SD_PGPDP_OBM, shows a 95.5% privacy score with lower time and space complexity, whereas the existing AA framework yields only 20.5% privacy score.
