AB  - This chapter establishes livestock metabolomics as an interactive information layer that interprets genomic potential, proteomic activity, microbiome co-metabolism, management inputs, and environmental pressures as high-information biochemical state signatures. It outlines ways artificial intelligence can be used to reduce this complexity to actionable intelligence through representation learning, graph- and pathway-aware modeling, causal and probabilistic inference, foundation models, and digital twin architectures. The chapter tackles the issues of incompleteness of annotations, the time nonstationarity, the heterogeneity of matrices, the uncertainty of low abundance, and the cross-cohort generalizability, focusing on the data engineering principles, ontologies, quality management, federation of collaboration, and lifecycle management. It puts together ethical, legal, and sustainability agendas, including equity, openness, data sovereignty, computational effectiveness, and sustainability. New horizons are ultra-high throughput and real-time sensing, isotope tracing fluxomics, spatial-temporal meta-atlases of metabolism, self-supervised annotation acceleration, causal digital twins, foundation multi-omics, and robust small-sample inference.
