AB  - This review synthesizes emerging evidence on co-planning as a foundational, relational stage of community-based research. While scholarship increasingly emphasizes Indigenous data sovereignty, Ethical Space, and knowledge co-production, practical guidance on how Indigenous communities, organizations, and researchers jointly initiate research remains limited. We synthesize contemporary conceptual developments and propose a co-planning framework grounded in reciprocity, shared decision-making, and relational stewardship. The review identifies five evidence-based co-planning steps: (1) building trust and relationships, (2) jointly identifying priorities, (3) co-designing research questions, (4) establishing shared governance, and (5) planning for relationship continuity. We also identify six recurring barriers (mistrust, institutional constraints, limited community capacity, power imbalances, data governance concerns, and logistical challenges) and highlight enabling strategies, including shared governance arrangements, inclusive engagement of women and youth, flexible agreements, and Indigenous data stewardship. We argue that co-planning is an iterative, adaptive process that supports equitable, stewardship-oriented research partnerships across the research lifecycle.
ER  - 

